WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:01.840
(pensive music)

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.390
- [Narrator] When
President Biden test drove

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:07.070
a new electric vehicle from
Ford, he liked what he saw.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.360
- This sucker's quick.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.130
- [Narrator] But the industry
has a problem to solve

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.433
before consumers get
onboard with EVs: charging.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.380
Experts say the concerns over charging

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.900
are holding many drivers back

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.880
from switching to EVs.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.300
That's sending companies on a race

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.850
to improve the nation's charging network.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.410
- It takes public fast
charging to sell cars.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.940
People need the confidence to know

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.590
that they're gonna be able to do that.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.300
- [Narrator] But building
this new fleet of chargers

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.540
won't be cheap.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.930
- There's very much a
chicken or the egg problem

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.620
because you'll put chargers in the ground

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.950
but there's just not demand.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:38.830
- [Narrator] Here's how charging

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.740
became the EV industry's bottleneck

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.000
and what some companies are going

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.413
to try and fix it.

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:45.910
(dramatic music)

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.040
Charging is a surprisingly complex issue

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.704
for the EV industry.

00:00:49.704 --> 00:00:51.190
One of the main challenges

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.360
is that in many cases, it takes too long

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.830
to fill up the battery of a car.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.450
The most satisfied EV owners,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.890
according to J.D. Power,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.720
are able to charge at home over night.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.760
That can be done with the
regular plugs in your home

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.890
but charging this way can take days

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.290
to full up a standard range vehicle.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.633
The industry calls this
level one charging.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:13.950
Homeowners can upgrade

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.370
to a level two charger

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.740
but that can cost thousands
of dollars to install

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:18.763
in some cases.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.830
Then there are drivers who
can't charge at home at all,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.400
like many who live in apartments

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.410
and rely on publicly available chargers.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.243
The research says

00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:30.053
that this network needs improvement.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.260
As of 2021, the US has roughly 41,000

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.930
of the faster level two stations available

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.570
to the public.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:39.870
The Department of Energy says

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.020
that by 2030, the US will need 600,000

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.523
of these machines to meet expected demand.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.980
Private companies like
Revel are stepping in

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.970
to try and get ahead of these issues

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.033
by installing even faster
level three stations.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.740
This is Revel's first super
hub in Brooklyn New York.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.280
It's stocked with a direct
current power station

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.123
to service level three chargers.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.963
The industry calls these DC-fast chargers.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.260
Paul Suhey, the company's co-founder,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.510
says that this site is the
largest fast charging depot

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.940
in the Americas.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.510
- New York City has a major problem

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.510
and the problem is charging infrastructure

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.130
just simply does not exist.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.460
It is a challenge to
find a studio apartment

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.460
in New York City,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.010
let alone a charging site

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.415
where you're talking about
skyscraper levels of power.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.370
- [Narrator] Revel says
that its fast charging plugs

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.520
can provide 100 miles of
range in about 20 minutes.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.640
The company believes
that its fast chargers

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.020
could ease the anxiety of EV ownership

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.210
for city dwellers who
typically have to pay

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.200
to enter a public parking garage

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.620
before paying again for their charge.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.860
- There's no pay wall.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.330
You can access a charger for free,

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.190
which is very unique in New York City.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.510
The second thing is just a massive site.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.150
We're talking 25 chargers in one location.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.450
It's just a much better user experience.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.450
- [Narrator] Revel isn't
the only company focused

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:02.423
on fast charging.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.790
DC-fast stations are the
quickest growing segment

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.323
of the EV charging market.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.810
Tesla pioneered this
technology in the 2010s

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.470
with its Supercharger network,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.490
which has more than
25,000 stations worldwide

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.670
but Tesla's system
doesn't work with hardware

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.310
from other manufacturers

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.270
and that represents another
problem with EV charging.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.113
Many of the systems on the
market are incompatible.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.120
In the US, companies are designing cars

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.060
that use different
types of charging plugs,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.910
which is creating problems
for people looking to fill up.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.130
That's fairly different
from the customer experience

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:40.610
at gas stations.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.810
In most cases, the nozzle at the pump

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.640
will fit the car.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.903
That won't necessarily
be the case for EVs.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.430
The industry is using
a standard plug shape

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.860
for level one and level two chargers

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.590
but there are three
different plug shapes in use

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.673
on the newer, faster chargers.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.460
Experts say that aside from Tesla,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.460
companies are moving toward
the combined charging system,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.390
or a CCS.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.130
But for now, drivers might need

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:08.710
to carry dongles to make sure

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:09.700
that their car works

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.600
with the charging ports
that they have find.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.653
That's a problem that companies
like EVgo want to solve.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:18.953
- Jonathan Levy, the company's
chief commercial officer

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:21.480
says that their technical team is working

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.060
to make their chargers compatible

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.300
with all EVs on the market.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.650
- We're currently in a process

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:27.799
where we're installing hundreds

00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:29.770
of integrated Tesla connectors

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.040
at EVgo fast chargers around the country

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.950
because they're more than half
the EVs being sold right now

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.330
and while other automakers
are bringing more models

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.450
to market and will continue to penetrate,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.633
Tesla drivers and EVs using CHAdeMO

00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:45.970
and EVs using CCS all
need access to charging.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:46.810
- [Narrator] The company plans

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.960
to have installed 600 Tesla connectors

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.573
across its fleet by the end of 2021.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.020
But in the race to make
these new, compatible,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.370
fast charging machines,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.260
the costs for companies and
consumers are adding up.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.270
Industry experts say that
building out a bigger

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.750
and faster fleet of chargers

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.150
is going to cost a lot of money upfront

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.753
before consumer demand picks up.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.870
Researchers say that an
installation of a level two charger

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.300
could cost tens of thousands of dollars.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.140
But costs for the newer DC-fast stations

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.423
could stretch into the
millions of dollars.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.540
To cover these costs, EVgo partnered

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.610
with a special purpose acquisition company

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.260
to quickly raise funds

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.600
but red tape is slowing down
the build out of its fleet.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.220
- Cities and counties have
really been hurt a lot

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.180
on a budget basis across the country,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.220
especially in COVID

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.520
and so if you have a part-time
permitting authority,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.620
that can really slow things down.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.320
When we think about how long it takes

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.140
to build a fast charging station,

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.030
it's anywhere from six to 18 months

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.720
with exceptions on either side of it

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.290
and it could be a lot quicker

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:49.620
if we were able to again
streamline some of this

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.510
and work on getting the flywheel spinning

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.736
in this ecosystem.

00:05:52.736 --> 00:05:53.569
(lively music)

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:55.320
- [Narrator] Some lawmakers
in Washington believe

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.330
that the industry needs help.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.058
- In answer to your direct
question, we have a deal.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.600
- [Narrator] A bipartisan group,

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.790
including the president and many senators

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.230
are calling for $7.5 billion

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.490
to build a network of
chargers along highways

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.530
and in rural areas.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.840
But even if public funding materializes,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.160
the EV charging companies

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.760
have to navigate other bottlenecks,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.380
like regulatory approvals

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.403
while the development costs pile up.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.390
That could leave some EV owners searching

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.015
for a place to plug in for some time.

00:06:24.015 --> 00:06:26.682
(pensive music)

